# Grammar calls an adverb that strengthens a claim an intensifier
# (`extremely`, `significantly`). Each one here raises a claim without
# measuring it. The fix drops the word (`significantly faster` becomes
# `faster`), or gives a number that stays true, such as a limit that a
# protocol sets. The Optional rule of the same name holds the
# intensifiers that sometimes carry meaning, such as `actually`.
#
# `significantly` also fires on a statistical claim (`differs
# significantly at p < 0.01`). No guard tells the two apart, and the
# adjective (`statistically significant`) passes.
#
# `highly available` names a redundant deployment, so `highly` skips a
# following `available`. `exponentially` stays out, because each of its
# 3 corpus hits states a growth rate (`increase exponentially by 2x`).
#
# It hit 85 lines in the measure.py corpus, and 19 of 20 sampled hits
# read as well without the word. The miss was `highly utilized nodes`,
# where the word carries the meaning.
expr main [extremely, incredibly, significantly, dramatically, drastically, greatly, vastly, tremendously, massively, hugely, substantially, considerably, enormously, immensely, exceedingly, (highly !available)]
let message "Intensifier. Delete the word, or give a number that stays true, such as a protocol limit."
let description "Flags adverbs that intensify a claim without measuring it."
let becomes ""
let kind "Style"
allows "The rewrite halves memory use."
allows "The difference is statistically significant at p < 0.05."
allows "The cluster is highly available."
